        "_id": "59a3892dd9eba100763a0f57",
        "background": "#3a3a3a",
        "content": "<LightImg \n    bg=\"#3a3a3a\"\n    src=\"https://s3-us-west-2.amazonaws.com/codex-content/uploads/images/memex.png\"\n    width=\"3200\"\n    height=\"2456\"\n></LightImg>\n<div class=\"indents\">\n    <p>\n        <span style=\"font-size:0.68em;letter-spacing:1px;\">BUT WHAT ABOUT THE NATURAL SCIENCES</span>, I would ask. Six weeks later, <span  style=\"font-size:0.68em;letter-spacing:1px;\">WE HAVE 4.530 ENTRIES ON THIS IN 190 SUBDIVISIONS</span>, came the reply. I would think about you and the natural sciences. Your response—a mute bond—would be already out-of-date, new entries inhaled through the <aa to=\"101,67,100\">breath of the telegraph</aa>. I would sit on top of my card cabinet fluttering the to date seventeen responses I had received, and kicking my legs.\n        <br/>\n        —<span style=\"font-size:0.68em;letter-spacing:1px;\">I WANT TO KNOCK THE CEILING WITH THE TOP OF MY HEAD</span>, I then sent. <br/>\n        —<span style=\"font-size:0.68em;letter-spacing:1px;\">THAT IS NOT HOW HEADS WORK. THEY ARE TOO COMPACT</span>, in response.</p>\n        <p>The operator on this end had drawn three question marks, under the message, on the card, in disbelief. The operator on this end had black hair and green eyes. With all who had taken part, I would not know where to focus my attention within the relay or as we may think. ∎\n    </p>\n</div>",
